WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.601 --> 00:00:03.302
-Our next guest is an Emmy-
nominated comedian and actress

00:00:03.302 --> 00:00:04.837
you know from her
Netflix comedy specials

00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:07.072
and shows like "Big Mouth,"
"Tuca &amp; Bertie,"

00:00:07.072 --> 00:00:08.575
"and Human Resources."

00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:11.611
She stars in "Beef," which
premieres on Netflix April 6th.

00:00:11.611 --> 00:00:12.745
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.745 --> 00:00:15.181
-Come on. Talk to me.

00:00:17.349 --> 00:00:21.120
-Okay, well, um,
you know, after the meeting,

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.024
I was sitting in the parking lot
thinking about how many meetings

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:28.160
and calls I've had to do
for the last two years.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.096
Still, somehow
there's no offer coming.

00:00:30.096 --> 00:00:32.631
And it just really
got to me, you know?

00:00:32.631 --> 00:00:35.834
So then I started driving,
and then there was this guy --

00:00:35.834 --> 00:00:37.937
-Hey, hey.
Before you spiral...

00:00:37.937 --> 00:00:40.673
I'm gonna have you stop
right there.

00:00:40.673 --> 00:00:42.208
Take a deep breath.

00:00:42.208 --> 00:00:45.611
[ Inhales deeply ]
Pause.

00:00:45.611 --> 00:00:49.882
You got to start focusing
on the positive. Okay?

00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:52.051
You know, maybe
we should start doing

00:00:52.051 --> 00:00:54.020
the gratitude journals again.

00:00:54.020 --> 00:00:55.888
-Please welcome back
to the show Ali Wong,

00:00:55.888 --> 00:00:57.356
everybody!

00:00:57.356 --> 00:00:59.425
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:59.425 --> 00:01:14.874 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:17.277
Welcome back, Ali.
-Thanks for having me.

00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:19.646
-I'm so thrilled to have you.
I know you were --

00:01:19.646 --> 00:01:21.914
Early days, you were in New York
working on standup.

00:01:21.914 --> 00:01:24.484
-I was -- I would do --
When I lived here, my God --

00:01:24.484 --> 00:01:27.753
and hustling, I would do up
to sometimes 13 sets a night.

00:01:27.753 --> 00:01:29.288
-That is insane to me.

00:01:29.288 --> 00:01:31.056
Bouncing all around the city?

00:01:31.056 --> 00:01:32.992
-Bouncing all around the city.
Sometimes to Brooklyn,

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:34.894
back to, like,
the Upper East Side,

00:01:34.894 --> 00:01:39.465
to, like, Bed-Stuy,
to the Lower East Side.

00:01:39.465 --> 00:01:42.768
And it was all for no money.
Sometimes three-minute sets.

00:01:42.768 --> 00:01:44.303
-And, now, when you were
doing the same --

00:01:44.303 --> 00:01:46.138
I'm assuming you're doing
the same three minutes

00:01:46.138 --> 00:01:49.075
13 different times,
13 different audiences.

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:50.209
-Yeah.
-Would they just be

00:01:50.209 --> 00:01:52.745
wildly different
as far as what worked where?

00:01:52.745 --> 00:01:55.147
-Yeah, but I wasn't really
paying attention to that.

00:01:55.147 --> 00:01:58.151
Like, I never paid attention
much to the audiences

00:01:58.151 --> 00:02:00.653
because they were so crappy.

00:02:00.653 --> 00:02:02.822
And a lot of the times
it was like,

00:02:02.822 --> 00:02:06.459
you know, like,
random crazy people

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:08.360
jerking off into their eggs.
-Yeah.

00:02:08.360 --> 00:02:10.062
-So it was like --
Who did not know

00:02:10.062 --> 00:02:12.332
there was a comedy show
going on.

00:02:12.332 --> 00:02:14.133
Oh, am I -- am I allowed
to say that about the eggs?

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:15.834
Oh, well. It's too late now.

00:02:15.834 --> 00:02:18.104
-Well, you know what?

00:02:18.104 --> 00:02:19.839
We are gonna get you
to ADR later --

00:02:19.839 --> 00:02:22.441
"Putting salt on their..."

00:02:22.441 --> 00:02:24.544
Real quick.
So I want to ask, though.

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:26.713
But you're getting no money,
and it doesn't seem like

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:28.113
you're getting helpful feedback.

00:02:28.113 --> 00:02:30.049
So what were you
getting out of it?

00:02:30.049 --> 00:02:32.619
-Well, it's more like
I have to pay attention

00:02:32.619 --> 00:02:34.920
when I say it out loud
to the energy.

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:37.590
And, like, if I --
if I have confidence,

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:39.792
even if I bomb, like,
it's still funny.

00:02:39.792 --> 00:02:41.894
That's really
valuable information.

00:02:41.894 --> 00:02:43.997
You know, like if I still
feel like, okay,

00:02:43.997 --> 00:02:45.965
even though this didn't
get much laughs,

00:02:45.965 --> 00:02:47.800
like if I still feel
attached to it,

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:50.202
that tells me something,
you know?

00:02:50.202 --> 00:02:53.539
-That -- I should also note
you make it look very easy,

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:56.342
your specials, and I think
it's a very nice thing for you

00:02:56.342 --> 00:02:58.878
to admit how hard you have to
work to make it look so easy.

00:02:58.878 --> 00:03:02.281
-Yeah, it was like it was --
And I still eat it a lot.

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:04.450
Like, when I go up at places,

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.419
people are always so excited
to see me,

00:03:06.419 --> 00:03:09.422
and then I sort of talk
in this very, like, hushed --

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:12.224
Like, I don't put a lot
of performance into it.

00:03:12.224 --> 00:03:13.993
And I talk in a sort of
monotone voice,

00:03:13.993 --> 00:03:16.028
and then I can see
people's, like, face --

00:03:16.028 --> 00:03:18.431
They're really excited,
and then it starts to fall,

00:03:18.431 --> 00:03:21.267
and they're like,
"Oh, my God. She's lost it."

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:24.470
And then -- But, again,
it's really about the writing.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.405
After a while,
it would get to the point

00:03:26.405 --> 00:03:28.207
where no matter where I went,

00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:31.143
those three minutes
would always still get laughs.

00:03:31.143 --> 00:03:33.079
-That's such
a fascinating process.

00:03:33.079 --> 00:03:34.580
-Yeah.
-I'm so happy

00:03:34.580 --> 00:03:36.482
you put yourself through it.

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:38.117
So then, though, I didn't
realize you moved back to L.A.

00:03:38.117 --> 00:03:40.319
You're doing all this work
on a comedy career,

00:03:40.319 --> 00:03:42.421
and then you go to L.A.
-Well, I went to L.A.

00:03:42.421 --> 00:03:44.757
actually to play
Christian Slater's assistant

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:46.592
in this sitcom,
and then I moved back

00:03:46.592 --> 00:03:50.429
to New York for like a year
to be on this medical drama.

00:03:50.429 --> 00:03:52.764
-Oh. So that was in New York.
-That was in New York.

00:03:52.764 --> 00:03:56.402
I played
an agoraphobic radiologist

00:03:56.402 --> 00:03:58.637
who had a love arc
with a plumber.

00:03:58.637 --> 00:04:00.139
-Oh, my God.
-Yeah.

00:04:00.139 --> 00:04:03.509
-You would be perfect for that.

00:04:03.509 --> 00:04:06.078
-And then, like, that was
the first dramatic role

00:04:06.078 --> 00:04:07.313
I ever had to do, kind of,

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:09.916
because I had --
It wasn't very funny,

00:04:09.916 --> 00:04:12.651
but I was always
trying to be funny,

00:04:12.651 --> 00:04:15.488
and the director was like,
"Stop trying to make

00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:17.389
'occipital lobe' funny!

00:04:17.389 --> 00:04:20.059
Just say what's going on
in the brain!"

00:04:20.059 --> 00:04:25.431
And then I -- The show
also had Vanessa Redgrave.

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.500
Sure. A legend.
-A legend.

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:31.170
And then I remember overhearing
one of the directors talk about

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.039
directing Vanessa Redgrave,

00:04:33.039 --> 00:04:34.974
and he was like,
"Oh, when she's on camera,

00:04:34.974 --> 00:04:37.543
it's like directing
a Rolls-Royce."

00:04:37.543 --> 00:04:38.878
And then I just walked away

00:04:38.878 --> 00:04:40.847
because I knew
if someone asked him,

00:04:40.847 --> 00:04:43.683
"What is it like to direct Ali?"
he would have said,

00:04:43.683 --> 00:04:47.519
"It's like directing
a Toyota Yaris."

00:04:47.519 --> 00:04:50.323
So I just left
because I was like,

00:04:50.323 --> 00:04:51.858
"I don't want my feelings hurt.'
-I'm gonna use

00:04:51.858 --> 00:04:53.959
the mention of cars
to segue very smoothly

00:04:53.959 --> 00:04:55.861
to this wonderful
new show, "Beef."

00:04:55.861 --> 00:04:58.364
This is about two people
who have a road-rage incident

00:04:58.364 --> 00:05:00.700
and then become obsessed
with each other.

00:05:00.700 --> 00:05:02.501
Excuse me.
Your costar, Steven Yeun.

00:05:02.501 --> 00:05:04.003
He's wonderful.
-He's amazing.

00:05:04.003 --> 00:05:06.905
-And this is a -- I mean,
there's very funny parts of it.

00:05:06.905 --> 00:05:08.741
It's also very dramatic
at times.

00:05:08.741 --> 00:05:10.376
-Yeah.
-And it's also very different.

00:05:10.376 --> 00:05:12.578
We were talking backstage.
It seems very unique to me.

00:05:12.578 --> 00:05:14.647
Did you enjoy it from the first
time you laid eyes on it?

00:05:14.647 --> 00:05:16.415
-So when, like, the creator,

00:05:16.415 --> 00:05:18.650
Lee Sung Jin,
came to me with Steven,

00:05:18.650 --> 00:05:20.753
I just really wanted to work
with those guys.

00:05:20.753 --> 00:05:23.055
That was, like, the biggest
priority. That was the biggest.

00:05:23.055 --> 00:05:24.757
Like, it was like,
"You had me at hello."

00:05:24.757 --> 00:05:26.625
Because I always wanted
to work with them.

00:05:26.625 --> 00:05:28.727
And then after they told me
about the concept,

00:05:28.727 --> 00:05:30.596
I was so excited,
and then when I read the pilot,

00:05:30.596 --> 00:05:34.066
I just read it on my toilet
in one sitting,

00:05:34.066 --> 00:05:36.268
and I had -- and my kids
kept on knocking on the door,

00:05:36.268 --> 00:05:38.437
and I was like, "Hold on,
hold on, hold on, hold on!"

00:05:38.437 --> 00:05:40.205
And I kept on, like,
scrolling through the iPad.

00:05:40.205 --> 00:05:42.007
And then I had that, like,
signature ring

00:05:42.007 --> 00:05:43.542
around my butt afterwards,

00:05:43.542 --> 00:05:47.012
and I was like, "Oh, this
is a good project. Yeah."

00:05:47.012 --> 00:05:49.315
-You called your agent and
you're like, "I got the ring."

00:05:49.315 --> 00:05:51.250
-Yeah, yeah. "I got the ring."

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.486
-And you --
Did you first meet Steven

00:05:53.486 --> 00:05:55.521
when he came
to see you do stand-up?

00:05:55.521 --> 00:05:56.923
-Yeah. He was so sweet.

00:05:56.923 --> 00:05:59.525
So, when my first stand-up
special, "Baby Cobra,"

00:05:59.525 --> 00:06:01.060
came out...
-Fantastic.

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:03.629
-Oh, thanks.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:03.629 --> 00:06:07.533
He sent a message to me on
Twitter about how great it was,

00:06:07.533 --> 00:06:10.035
and he encouraged all of
his followers to watch it.

00:06:10.035 --> 00:06:14.440
And then he came, and I think
he even paid money to --

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:15.942
for him
and a bunch of his friends

00:06:15.942 --> 00:06:19.679
to come see me
at the Ace in downtown L.A.

00:06:19.679 --> 00:06:22.748
-Did you know he was coming?
-I think I knew he was coming.

00:06:22.748 --> 00:06:24.684
And then he came backstage
afterwards.

00:06:24.684 --> 00:06:27.019
And then one of his friends
kept on taking pictures of me,

00:06:27.019 --> 00:06:28.987
and I was like,
"Hey, man, what are you doing?!"

00:06:28.987 --> 00:06:31.423
And Steven was like,
"This is my friend.

00:06:31.423 --> 00:06:33.392
He's a portrait artist."

00:06:33.392 --> 00:06:36.161
And that guy we ended up casting
as the husband that you see

00:06:36.161 --> 00:06:37.930
in the clip.
-That was in the clip.

00:06:37.930 --> 00:06:39.264
-Yeah.
-So there you go.

00:06:39.264 --> 00:06:41.533
-He plays an artist
in the show, and then he's --

00:06:41.533 --> 00:06:44.003
he's a real-life, incredible,
incredible painter, yeah.

00:06:44.003 --> 00:06:46.238
-Well, that's fantastic.
It's really a great show.

00:06:46.238 --> 00:06:47.940
And it's so lovely
to have you here.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:50.376
Thanks for being back with us.
-Thanks so much for having me.

00:06:50.376 --> 00:06:53.378
-Ali Wong, everybody. "Beef"
premieres on Netflix April 6th.

00:06:53.378 --> 00:06:55.581
We'll be right back with music
from The Hold Steady.

